\end{frame}


\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{Coupled energy and mass balance}

  Organisms must simultaneously satisfy energy and mass balance:

  \vfill

  \[
    \dot{Q}_{solar} + \dot{Q}_{IR,in} + \dot{Q}_{metab} =
    \dot{Q}_{IR,out} + \dot{Q}_{conv} + \dot{Q}_{evap} + \dot{Q}_{cond} + \dot{Q}_{s}
  \]

  \vfill

  \begin{columns}[T]
    \column{0.48\textwidth}
    \textbf{Energy balance} determines:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item body temperature (ectotherms)
      \item metabolic rate (endotherms)
      \item activity and performance
    \end{itemize}

    \column{0.48\textwidth}
    \textbf{Mass balance} determines:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item water status
      \item energy reserves
      \item growth and reproduction
    \end{itemize}
  \end{columns}

  \vfill

  \centering
  \tiny Based on \textcite{kearney2009}

\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{The fundamental niche from first principles}

  \begin{itemize}
    \item An organism cannot survive where it would be in negative energy
      or mass balance
    \item Reproductive capacity depends on ``profits'' in these currencies
    \item The set of environmental conditions where energy and mass
      balance is positive defines the \textbf{fundamental niche}
  \end{itemize}

  \vfill

  \uncover<2->{
    \begin{block}{Key insight}
      The fundamental niche can be \textbf{computed} from first principles ---
      it is not merely a statistical description of where a species is found.
    \end{block}
  }

\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{From functional traits to climate space}

  An organism's functional traits (morphology, physiology, behaviour)
  determine how it experiences a given set of environmental conditions:

  \vfill

  \begin{columns}[T]
    \column{0.48\textwidth}
    \textbf{Functional traits:}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item body size and shape
      \item solar reflectivity (albedo)
      \item skin permeability
      \item metabolic rate
      \item behavioural repertoire
    \end{itemize}

    \column{0.48\textwidth}
    \textbf{Environmental axes:}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item air temperature
      \item solar radiation
      \item wind speed
      \item humidity
      \item substrate temperature
    \end{itemize}
  \end{columns}

  \vfill

  \uncover<2->{
    The energy balance equation translates environmental conditions into
    body temperature --- the key physiological variable for ectotherms.
  }

\end{frame}

